# SYNOPSIS

```text
size[4] Tshare tag[2] fid[4] holds[4]
size[4] Rshare tag[2] token[16]

size[4] Tjoin tag[2] newfid[4] token[16]
size[4] Rjoin tag[2] qid[13] iounit[4]

size[4] Tseek tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] whence[1]
size[4] Rseek tag[2] offset[8]

size[4] Tdesc tag[2] fid[4] flags[4]
size[4] Rdesc tag[2]
```

# DESCRIPTION

These four are 9Px's own, with no counterpart in 9P2000 or 9P2000.L, and belong to the `posix` extension: a server answers them only once `Tversion` has negotiated `+posix` (see version(5)).
They give a POSIX program what its descriptors need of a file server: an offset and an append flag kept by the server, not the client, and shared by a process and its children, across `fork` and `exec`, though each child has a connection of its own.
Their type numbers are 150 to 157.

## Open files

With `posix`, a server keeps an open file for each fid it opens that is not a directory: the node, the open mode, an offset starting at 0, and whether writes append.
`Topen`'s mode bit `0x80`, `P9_OAPPEND`, which a server takes out of the mode before its file system sees it and ignores without `posix`, makes the open file append from the start (see open(5)).
A `Tread` or `Twrite` whose offset is all ones, `P9_OFFSET_CURRENT`, reads or writes at the open file's offset and moves it on by the count; at any other offset it does as 9P2000 does and leaves the offset alone (see read(5)).
A write at the current offset of a file that appends goes to the file's end, found at that moment; since a server answers one request at a time, the append is atomic.
An open file lasts while a fid is open on it, or while it has holds, as below.
A server's open files are one table, 256 entries, shared by all its connections; an open that finds it full fails with `out of memory`.

## Share and join

The `Tshare` request asks for a token for the open file of <fid>, which must be open on a file that is not a directory.
The token is good for <holds> joins, 1 to 64, and the open file holds at most 64 at once; `Rshare` returns it.
The token is 16 bytes from the server's random generator, made at the open file's first `Tshare` and the same for every later one.
A server whose spawn message gave it no `entropy` (see svc(6)) has no generator, and refuses `Tshare`: a token that could be guessed would let any connection reach an open file it could not open.

The `Tjoin` request, on any connection to the same server, makes <newfid> a fid open on the open file the token names, and uses up one of its holds.
The new fid shares everything of the open file: its offset, its appending and its mode; it is not walked, has no attach root of its own to walk from, and is open from the start.
The server opens the file again as a join, not as a new open, so permissions are not checked again and a file removed since is still reached: the token is the authority.
`Rjoin` returns the file's <qid> and an <iounit> of the session's msize less 24, as `Ropen`'s.
Tokens are compared whole, in constant time.

Holds last 10 seconds from the last `Tshare` of the file, or from its last fid's clunk, whichever is later.
So a child may join after its parent has closed the file or exited, as an `exec` does at once; holds not used by then run out, open file or not, so a client that gave out tokens no child took cannot fill the file's 64.
An open file with no fid and no live hold is gone, and its token with it.

The musl back end shares each open file before a `fork` and writes the token into the child's spawn record; the child joins on the connection its namespace gives the file's path, or its directory's, and opens the file again at the parent's offset if the join fails.
Its `poll` shares a file with itself, a token of one hold, to join it on a second connection where a read can wait.

## Seek

The `Tseek` request moves the open file's offset of <fid>, as `lseek` does, and `Rseek` returns where it now is.
<whence> is 0 to set it to <offset>, 1 to add <offset> to it, and 2 to add <offset> to the file's length, which the server asks its file system for then.
<offset> is read as signed, two's complement.
A result below 0, or past the largest signed 64-bit number, and a <whence> above 2 are refused, and the offset is left as it was.

## Desc

The `Tdesc` request sets the open file's flags, as `fcntl(F_SETFL)` sets a descriptor's.
Bit 1 of <flags>, append, makes writes at the current offset go to the file's end, and its absence makes them write at the offset again; the other bits are ignored.
It changes every fid that shares the open file.

## Errors

Before `posix` is negotiated, and from a server without the shared table, each of these is answered `operation not supported`; so is `Tshare` on a server with no generator.
A <fid> that is not one of the connection's is `unknown fid`.
A <fid> that is not open, or is open on a directory, or got no open file because the table was full, is `fid already in use`, though no fid is.
A token that names no live open file with a hold left is `file does not exist`, and a <newfid> already in use `fid already in use`.
`Tshare`'s <holds> of 0, over 64, or more than the file has room for is `offset out of range`; `Tseek`'s refusals are `bad message`.
VectraOS's client sends none of the four unless the session has `posix`, and fails the call with `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` itself.

